Every MUST SHOW item is on the page: the affirmative provenance banner (badge, `aztec-testnet`, endpoint, capture moment, 63629–63678 replay window), the identity bar with a real hash, block 63675, an exit that lands on the chain, stepping controls, a 128/345 readout and the phase rail; three panes that each explain the missing source in their own words; a `Supply sources` route out; and the replay telemetry with `effectsMismatched: 0`. No invented source, no fixture leakage, no failure banner, no dangling cost operand, no NOIR tag. The shell fits 1080.

Through the density lens this is the weak capture. The product records 345 instruction indices and is currently at 128, yet the 910×805 Code pane shows none of them — it holds four lines of prose, a redundant fifth, a button, and 555px of blank. Two thirds of a 1920×1080 viewport carry nine lines of text while the one pane with data, 380px wide, wraps a 32-byte fee across three lines and clips its JSON at its bottom edge. The extra width this viewport offers is spent entirely on empty panes.

Highest-priority fixes: render the recorded program-counter stream in the Code pane with 128 marked — the copy already says only the text is missing — and reallocate width to the Transaction pane so the fee and telemetry stop wrapping.

